  {
    "sha": "b189c960dd653776c8b1108adc0998e762cb16dc",
    "node_id": "C_kwDOVmlK7doAKGIxODljOTYwZGQ2NTM3NzZjOGIxMTA4YWRjMDk5OGU3NjJjYjE2ZGM",
    "commit": {
      "author": {
        "name": "Mike Kinney",
        "email": "mike.kinney@gmail.com",
        "date": "2026-11-08T00:03:37Z"
      },
      "committer": {
        "name": "Mike Kinney",
        "email": "mike.kinney@gmail.com",
        "date": "2026-11-08T00:03:37Z"
      },
      "message": "fix(post): 770MX travel bands in program coordinates, full stroke each way\n\nA .mch travel band is a stroke in PROGRAM coordinates, relative to the\nwork offset (assets/machines/AGENTS.md); the machine check subtracts the\nG54 origin. The 770MX file stated machine-style strokes (Z -13.25..0,\nX -7..7, Y -3.75..3.75 in), so every clearance move above G54 Z0 was a\nlimit problem: the first real job through the GUI (Face + 2D Contour)\nrefused to post with \"40 limit problem(s) ... the stroke is\n-336.550..0.000\". Where G54 sits inside the envelope is the operator's\nchoice, so the band is now the full published stroke each way (X +-14,\nY +-7.5, Z +-13.25 in): anything outside it is unreachable wherever G54\nsits, and ordinary programs pass.\n\nPinned by tormach_post::a_face_job_on_the_built_in_770mx_passes_the_gate_and_posts_inch.\n\nRefs #131\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>",
